<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lakkundi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lakkundi is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadag">Gadag</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, that was a prosperous town and mint under the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> in the 11th and 12th centuries. It holds dozens of temples and stepped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> in the ornate later Chalukya style, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> Jinalaya, a Jain temple endowed by the noblewoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attimabbe">Attimabbe</a>, and the Kasivishveshvara temple.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lakkundi.jpg" alt="Lakkundi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P. Madhusudan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:11th_century_Kasivisvesvara_temple,_Lakkundi,_Karnataka_India_-_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
